### Step 4 — Security & Compliance Hardening

#### 4a. API Key Management

```bash
# Never use .env files in production
# Use AWS Secrets Manager:
aws secretsmanager create-secret \
  --name "medibot/production" \
  --secret-string '{"PINECONE_API_KEY":"...","OPENAI_API_KEY":"..."}'

# Reference in ECS Task Definition:
{
  "secrets": [
    {"name": "PINECONE_API_KEY", "valueFrom": "arn:aws:secretsmanager:..."},
    {"name": "OPENAI_API_KEY",   "valueFrom": "arn:aws:secretsmanager:..."}
  ]
}
```

#### 4b. Data Encryption

| Layer | Current State | Required Action |
|---|---|---|
| Data in Transit | Nginx TLS configured | ✅ Ensure TLS 1.3 only in production |
| Data at Rest (DB) | SQLite unencrypted | Enable RDS encryption at rest (AES-256) |
| Data at Rest (Redis) | Unencrypted | Enable ElastiCache encryption at rest |
| Logs | Plain text `flask.log` | Enable CloudWatch with encryption |
| Backup | Not configured | Enable RDS automated backups (7-day PITR) |
| S3 (if ever used) | N/A | Enable SSE-S3 encryption by default |

#### 4c. HIPAA Compliance Checklist (US Users)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> If you serve US users who enter Protected Health Information (PHI), HIPAA applies.

- [ ] Sign a **Business Associate Agreement (BAA)** with AWS before using any AWS services
- [ ] Sign a BAA with OpenAI (available via enterprise plan) or OpenRouter (check their BAA policy)
- [ ] Sign a BAA with Pinecone (available on Enterprise tier)
- [ ] **Implement audit logging** — every access to medical data must be logged with user ID, timestamp, and action
- [ ] **Right to Access** — users must be able to download all their conversation data (`/export` endpoint)
- [ ] **Right to Deletion** — users must be able to delete their account and all associated data
- [ ] **Minimum Necessary** — stop storing full user message text if not needed for core function; store intent + response only
- [ ] Enable VPC isolation for all services (no public RDS endpoint)
- [ ] Enable AWS CloudTrail and GuardDuty for threat detection
- [ ] Annual HIPAA risk assessment documentation

#### 4d. India DPDP Act 2023 Compliance Checklist (Primary Target Audience)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 has enforcement implications even for apps that process Indian citizens' data.

- [ ] **Consent before data collection** — mandatory pre-chat consent popup with explicit purpose statement
- [ ] **Data Localization** — store data in `ap-south-1` (Mumbai) AWS region; do not transfer data outside India without explicit consent
- [ ] **Data Fiduciary registration** — register as a Data Fiduciary with DPDA when rules are notified
- [ ] **Data Retention limits** — implement automatic deletion of conversations older than 90 days (configurable)
- [ ] **Right to Erasure** — `DELETE /account` endpoint that hard-deletes all user data
- [ ] **Grievance Officer** — appoint a Grievance Officer and publish their contact on Privacy page
- [ ] **Privacy Notice in Hindi** — ensure Privacy Policy is available in Hindi (DPDP requirement for Indian users)
- [ ] **No data sharing with third parties** without consent — review OpenRouter's data retention policies
- [ ] Implement **Purpose Limitation** — data collected for medical queries cannot be used for marketing

